during the second century AD the known  world sorry America's was flanked by two  great empires to the west set the Roman  Empire and to the east lay the Han  Dynasty both of these powers sat at the  end of the Silk Road trade route which  saw Goods move mostly from China to roam  across continents and both of these  empires had a great deal in common they  were geographically large they were run  by emperors they had professional armies  and they were plagued by the barbarian  neighbors this obviously raises the  question what did these two know about  each other surely Rome and China had  some idea of each other's existence and  maintained some sort of contact so first  of all both the Roman Empire and Han  China were aware of each other and both  knew that the other was a great Empire  the Chinese mostly new Rome as de Keene  which means great China implying that  China saw Rome as its equal on the other  side of the world and the Romans knew  China by the name Sara Kura much like  how they saw everyone else they saw them  as utterly inferior the most obvious  contact they kept with each other was by  a trade through India the Persian Empire  revived the northern trade routes across  the Black Sea that's not to say it was a  one-way though the Chinese imported a  great deal from Rome most notably glass  the Chinese court was stuffed with Roman  luxury goods and it was a mark of  extreme wealth to own Roman glassware  and this is actually one of the reasons  that the Roman Empire was often referred  to as the treasure country by Chinese  bureaucrats as for the Roman idea of  China well it was much less flattering  partly due to Roman ideals surrounding  stoicism the import of luxuries like  silk and precious stones were seen as  immoral and decadent and given the roman  dislike for running a trade deficit  china was seen as a hostile Empire even  if it was a world away and in fact many  Romans believed that it was Rome's fate  to one day conquer China anyway beyond  economic ties how did Roman China  interact of the two great empires China  was the first to attempt to establish  former diplomatic contact and this was  when a man called Daniel was tasked with  travelling to Rome now he never actually  got there partially because it took  years of travel time and also because  his travels took him through the Persian  Empire and the Persians told him how he  would likely never return home if he  attempted to reach Rome as a result he  turned back and the Persians gave a sigh  of relief since they didn't want China  and Rome to become friends after ying  the hand reported that an ambassador of  qing andin which is likely either  Antoninus pious or marcus aurelius  Antoninus arrived in china in 166 ad  however the evidence of this is sketchy  and there's no certainty of whether or  not the two held formal relations and in  fact they simply could have been traders  and so all too  there isn't enough concrete evidence to  assert that whether or not the two great  empires made any sort of concerted  effort to talk to one another  the Chinese certainly took a greater  interest in the Romans than the Romans  did in them but this didn't mean that  the Romans knew nothing of Han China  they knew it was a great Empire of a men  12 whose exports affected both the Roman  economy and also Roman morality whereas  the Chinese knew that Rome was also a  grand Empire in a land of immense  treasures as such we know that they  certainly 100% knew of each other's  existence and traded a great deal I hope  you enjoyed this episode and thank you  for watching but thanks to my patreon  supporters whom you can see on screen  now and with an extra special thanks to  James Bissonnette Ike Danny Maloney  calling Castleman Marvin Castle Rob  Waterhouse Aaron the white Jordan long  way Chris wicker Gustav Swann Gareth  Turner  Marcus Ozma Maggie packs Kowski David  Silverman spinning three plates  Spencer Lightfoot Lexi Schwinn Winston  Cawood Kelly money